WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.593
What does a robot need to lift a cup by its handle? A video of the right grasp, and the actions that make it work.

2
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.095
We want one hand on the handle and the other hand free.

3
00:00:13.395 --> 00:00:19.005
With text alone, a second hand steadies the cup. With the sketch, one hand lifts it by the handle.

4
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:29.297
Even when the video shows the intended grasp, fingers and bottle can overlap on screen while remaining apart in depth.

5
00:00:29.597 --> 00:00:32.371
Copy that pose, and the robot can close on air.

6
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.124
This bottle is eighteen centimeters tall. Its size and path in the sketch guide the generated pour.

7
00:00:39.424 --> 00:00:44.437
To recover the robot action, we fit the fingers to that same surface as the bottle moves.

8
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:55.260
Given the pouring task, the agent uses RGB and depth to lay out the tabletop, an eighteen-centimeter bottle, and a fixed receiving cup.

9
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:05.044
The agent chooses the lower bottle wall and a clear approach for the right hand. The palm sphere stops where its surface touches the bottle.

10
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:13.143
The agent sets waypoints to clear the cup, tilt over its opening, and put the bottle back.

11
00:01:13.443 --> 00:01:19.139
The compiler connects them, checks reach and clearance, and renders the motion from the original camera.

12
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.353
The sphere guides the palm. The moving bottle shows where and when to tilt.

13
00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:29.768
The video model fills in the fingers and the scene appearance.

14
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.839
Move the bowl, and the hand takes a different path.

15
00:01:35.139 --> 00:01:41.304
With the sketch, the share of videos that follow the requested path rises from nine to seventy percent.

16
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:48.273
The hand in the video may tilt the bottle early. Dynamic time warping matches the palm to the sketch sphere.

17
00:01:48.573 --> 00:01:51.581
Each video frame inherits the matching bottle pose.

18
00:01:53.300 --> 00:02:00.799
The video identifies skin patches likely to touch. We bind those patches to the lower bottle wall specified in the sketch.

19
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:06.789
Blue lines measure the gap to the bottle surface. Purple marks where the reconstructed fingers pass inside.

20
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.673
First we fit one hand scale for the whole clip, and a wrist pose for each frame.

21
00:02:11.973 --> 00:02:15.323
Then we fix the scale and adjust the wrist and finger angles.

22
00:02:15.623 --> 00:02:22.087
The fingers should match the image and stay on the same bottle patch as it moves, with less gap and penetration.

23
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:30.828
At grasp time, we solve for the robot’s arm and finger joints together, starting from the refined human grasp.

24
00:02:31.128 --> 00:02:39.789
The active pads should reach their targets and face the bottle surface. We also penalize penetration and keep the palm close to the human reference.

25
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:46.791
From its own grasp, the robot follows the change in the human wrist pose. Each image has a matching joint command.

26
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:52.804
The drawer slides straight back. The lid rotates around its hinge as the hand presses it shut.

27
00:02:54.300 --> 00:03:00.529
For placement, the robot lets go over the tray. For extraction, it keeps hold of the tissue as it lifts.

28
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.695
Physics replay tests the grasp: does the sheet come out of the box with the fingers?

29
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:14.465
Sketch achieves the highest closed-loop success. New objects are harder for the policy than new layouts.

30
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:23.276
Sketch synthesizes grasps and motions for different arms and hands, from a parallel gripper to multi-fingered hands.

31
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:32.337
On the physical robot, the policy uses camera observations to grasp the bottle and pour into the bowl.

32
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:41.356
The robot picks up a brush, stacks cups, grasps a cup handle, and puts paper in the bin.

33
00:03:41.656 --> 00:03:46.392
Closing the drawer and laptop are separately trained and evaluated extensions.

34
00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:55.466
Thank you for watching.
